#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92dd864a-8bc0-547c-84c9-ffd562909315" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Matrices When They Are Conformable"}] :question/id #uuid "66ec04a0-2af8-5187-98b2-b06eedd5b0a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4406" :question/problem "Find $CD$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D$, so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{D} = \\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD$, one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, & -4 = 1 \\cdot 0 + (-2) \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, & 1 = 1 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, & -1 = 2 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, & 4 = 2 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71ff07eb-2e16-529e-b17c-1bf7cb9703bd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "3a696ed3-e341-5ea6-bf17-681ba8e386c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4407" :question/problem "Let $C=AB$. What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $A$ by the $2$nd column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{32} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100655 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d7d67c6-d54f-5ee1-af59-fd63d6687cfd" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c3ed8d05-758d-5ae0-bf57-3779fde6020b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5885" :question/problem "Compute the product\n$\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {-1 \\ 0 \\ -2} \\right\\rbrack \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {2 & -5} \\right\\rbrack$." :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {-1 \\ 0 \\ -2} \\right\\rbrack \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {2 & -5} \\right\\rbrack &= \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {(-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5) \\ 0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5)\\ (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)} \\right\\rbrack \\[5pt] &= \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {-2 & 5 \\ 0 & 0 \\ -4 & 10} \\right\\rbrack}\n$$"}]}
